<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cecina (river)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cecina is a river of western Tuscany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, about 80 km long, rising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colline-metallifere">Colline Metallifere</a> and flowing west to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrrhenian-sea">Tyrrhenian Sea</a> at the town of Cecina. Its valley, below the ridge of Volterra, has salt springs and formed the heart of the territory of the Etruscan city of Volaterrae.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cecina-river.jpg" alt="Cecina (river)" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Larth_Rasnal · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cecina_river.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
